The scenario numbers:

```go
func handler(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
    // Each request IS a goroutine (net/http spawns one per connection)
    data, err := slowService(r.Context())  // this blocks the GOROUTINE, not the thread
    json.NewEncoder(w).Encode(data)
}

http.ListenAndServe(":8080", mux)
// 10,000 concurrent requests:
//   - 10,000 goroutines (2KB each = ~20MB — fine)
//   - GOMAXPROCS OS threads (16 cores = 16 threads actually RUNNING Go code)
//   - Blocked-on-I/O goroutines park (the thread handles others)
//   - The DB and the downstream service see the bottleneck, not the server
```

The scheduler's mechanics for this scenario: 10,000 G (goroutines) distributed across 16 P (scheduler contexts, one per core). Each P has a local run queue. A goroutine blocking on the DB call parks in the network poller — the M (thread) it was running on picks the next G from the P's queue. The 16 threads are always busy with the goroutines that are actually runnable.

The contrast with thread-per-request (Java's classic model / C's): 10,000 threads × 1MB stack = 10GB reserved; context-switching across 10,000 threads = the scheduler thrash; the OS caps (ulimit) hit far earlier. The goroutine model makes the concurrency level a non-issue — the SERVER's bottleneck moves to where it should be (the DB, the downstream).

The genuine limits to name in an interview: the downstream resources. 10,000 concurrent requests = 10,000 DB connections (the DB pool — max 25 connections with queuing), 10,000 open files (the fd limit), 10,000 DNS lookups. Go removes the language-level limit; the system's other limits remain.
